---
id: bundle-help
title: bundle
---

creates a self-contained, air-gapped bundle of one or more ```sh nuts``` applications and their dependencies

##) SYNOPSIS:

```sh bundle <id>...```
        creates a bundle for the given application id(s) and their dependencies

##) OPTIONS:

```sh --app <id>```
        adds the given application id to the bundle ; equivalent to passing ```sh <id>``` as a non-option argument (may be repeated)

```sh --lib <id>```
        adds the given id to the bundle as a plain library (its dependencies are resolved but it is not treated as an executable application ; may be repeated)

```sh --jar, --as-jar```
        builds the bundle as a single executable jar file (default format)

```sh --zip, --as-zip```
        builds the bundle as a zip archive

```sh --dir, --as-dir```
        builds the bundle as a plain folder (no ```sh nuts``` bundle metadata files are written)

```sh --exploded, --as-exploded```
        builds the bundle as an unpacked folder, keeping the ```sh nuts``` bundle metadata files so it can later be cleaned or rebuilt in place

```sh --target <path>```
        sets the destination file (for ```sh --jar```/```sh --zip```) or folder (for ```sh --dir```/```sh --exploded```) ; when omitted, a name is derived from the resolved application name and version

```sh --clean```
        (with ```sh --dir``` or ```sh --exploded``` only) deletes any bundle content and metadata already present at the target location before building

```sh --app-name, --name <name>```
        overrides the application name used to derive the default bundle file/folder name

```sh --app-version <version>```
        overrides the application version used to derive the default bundle file/folder name

```sh --app-title, --title <title>```
        sets the application title stored in the bundle info

```sh --app-desc, --desc <description>```
        sets the application description stored in the bundle info

```sh --optional```
        marks resolved dependencies as optional

```sh --embedded```
        runs the embedded ```sh nuts``` workspace (bootstrapped when the bundle is executed) in embedded mode

```sh -y, --yes```
        answers yes to confirmation prompts of the embedded workspace when the bundle is executed

```sh -z, --reset```
        resets the embedded ```sh nuts``` workspace when the bundle is executed

```sh -l, --verbose```
        runs the embedded ```sh nuts``` workspace in verbose mode when the bundle is executed

```!include classpath:/net/thevpc/nuts/runtime/includes/standard-command.ntf```

##) EXAMPLES:

```sh bundle my-group:my-app#1.0```
        creates an executable jar bundle (```sh my-app-1.0-bundle.jar```) containing ```sh my-group:my-app#1.0``` and its dependencies

```sh bundle my-group:my-app#1.0 --zip --target my-app-bundle.zip```
        creates a zip bundle at ```sh my-app-bundle.zip```

```sh bundle my-group:my-app#1.0 --exploded --target ./my-app-bundle --clean```
        builds (or refreshes) an exploded bundle folder, wiping any previous content first

```sh bundle my-group:my-app#1.0 --lib my-group:extra-plugin#2.0```
        bundles the app together with an extra library that is not itself launched as an application

